  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nCloudy, mild, and wet conditions will continue this afternoon as\na large area of light rain and drizzle gradually spreads north \nacross the region. The precipitation will gradually diminish\ntonight into Monday, but the low overcast conditions are likely\nto linger through at least Monday. The upside is that\ntemperatures will be fairly steady in the mid to upper 50s\ntonight which is 10-15 degrees above normal.\n\nOur next storm system will impact the Quad State Tuesday through\nWednesday as a north/south oriented band of showers moves slowly\neastward across the region ahead of a cold front. Elevated\ninstability remains very limited and focused more over \nsoutheast Missouri early on Tuesday, but as the band moves east\naway from the upper trough, any thunder should diminish. \nRainfall amounts remain in the 1\" to 2\" range with the highest \namounts expected in the south and west and the least in the \nnortheast. The NBM has a 70-90% chance of at least 1\" of \nrainfall over most of the region, with the exception being \nsouthwest Indiana where the chance is only 50%. The probability \nof 2\" is very low at this time.\n \nPrecipitable water values 200-300% of normal or near the 99th \npercentile of climatology, coupled with a slow moving band or \nrain, certainly creates a concern for heavy rainfall, and some \nminor flooding issues cannot be ruled out mainly Tuesday and \nTuesday night. If the upper system were to trend farther south, \ncloser to the Quad State, the increased instability could result\nin more of a heavy rainfall and flooding threat, and possibly \neven a severe storm threat. We will keep an eye on it.\n\nAnother aspect of the Tuesday storm system will be strong south\nwinds. It looks like the strongest winds will be from the \nbootheel region north northeast through Carbondale and Mt Vernon\nIllinois, and they may be focused in the morning. The NBM which\ntends to underperform on windy days only has a 20-60% chance of\nwind gusts exceeding 30 mph over southeast Missouri and \nsouthern Illinois, but the 00Z NAEFS/ECENS have a 50-90% chance \nthroughout the area. The 12Z REFS, however, has a 90% chance of \nreaching 30 mph over the entire area, and a 30-50% chance of \nreaching 40 mph over the favored portions of southeast Missouri \nand southern Illinois. We will continue to monitor the situation\nfor a potential Wind Advisory for some portion of the region \nTuesday.\n\nBehind the cold front, the entire region should be dry by \nWednesday night, and temperatures should drop to near normal for\nFriday into next weekend.",
